{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>וירא.</b> An unusual form, because the yod is opened with patach and the word is from the kal binyan, and after the yod there is none of the guttural letters, which is the rule for expanding what precedes them. Nor is it proper for a person to drop the vav, or to say thus together with the companions of the yod, which are the signs of the servants [i.e., the shva-na letters]. In my opinion, when they wished to form it from ראה, on the pattern of וַיִשְבְ ממנו שבי (Numbers 21:1), the he of ראה would be missing, and also it is not possible for the alef to appear at the end of the word, and so what remains is chirik under the yod, and its pattern would be like וייף בגדלו (Ezekiel 31:7), and it would intermingle with the pattern of ירה. Therefore they opened [with patach] the yod:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The phrase 'חברי היו\"ד שהם סימני המשרתים' refers to the shva-na letters that follow a yod with chirik; resolved per standard grammatical terminology."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:24.335944609Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}